Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
hey there guys thanks for tuning in so  today i'm going to try something a  little bit different i'm going to be  doing a a focus a highlight spotlight  one of my favorite exercises that being  the bodyweight row and i'm going to talk  to you about why it's something i think  belongs in everybody's arsenal and  everybody's routine but it doesn't  really get the love that it deserves so  basically the bodyweight row is an  exercise that involves lying underneath  something that's raised slightly off the  ground holding onto it with your arms  keeping your heels on the ground torso  rigid and then pulling your upper body  up towards the bar or the um gymnastics  rings or whatever else that you've  chosen to use this is sometimes also  called a inverted push-up the reason  being of course that it is literally  like a push-up but upside down you're  pulling yourself up instead of down but  you're using the same kind of 70 of your  own body weight pulling up just that  upper part of your torso this right away  is one of the things that makes it so  useful it's the perfect counter to the  push-up if you're doing lots and lots of  push-ups then you can also incorporate  lots and lots of body weight rows and  that's going to do wonders for you  that's going to balance out your  physique nicely because you can do a lot  more bodyweight rows than you can do  pull ups or chin ups and this is also  another reason it's so useful because a  lot of people actually can't do a single  pull up or a single chin up this is a  fantastic progression towards that so if  you can't do a pull-up then you can just  do half of a pull-up by performing a  bodyweight row there's also plenty of  variations to make it even easier for  example you can stand up in front of a  wall hold on to something that's  attached to the wall say maybe a rope  that's connected to a door handle and  then lean back and pull yourself towards  it so you're using less resistance and  if you find it too easy conversely you  can raise your legs onto something  higher up and that way make the movement  more difficult this also works perfectly  as a mechanical drop set for the pull up  or chin up so you can perform as many  pull ups or chin ups as you can and as  soon as you can't perform a single rep  more you drop down to the body weight  row and continue oh and you also don't  need much in the way of equipment if  you've just got a big heavy table you  can hang onto the bottom of that and  perform bodyweight rows there a  fantastic way to train your lats and  your biceps if you don't have access to  dumbbells or a chin up bar and again  you'd be surprised how many people ask  me about that you can also use as i've  said gymnastics rings if you want to add  a stability factor i like using my dip  bars which i keep right behind my desk  and i perform in between work you can  also do it with two sticks holding onto  either of them on either side you can  wrap the towel around a pull-up bar  there's so many ways to do this exercise  it's something i really think that  pretty much everyone has access to which  you can't say about most exercises  if you're in the gym then just set the  bar low and the squat rack and you can  use that to hold on to instead and that  also allows you to adjust the height  really nicely oh that's great but it's  not the real reason that i love the  bodyweight row the great thing about  bodywork very first of all is that it is  a horizontal pulling movement and that's  something that we don't get enough of in  the gym in my opinion and especially if  you're training predominantly with  calisthenics you've got lots of vertical  pulling motions but not many horizontal  ones pulling in towards your torso  this is a useful movement that we use in  real life whether it's opening a door or  pulling a rope and a tug of war and so  it's something that's useful to train as  well  at the same time though the real power  of this movement comes from the fact  that it's also a fantastic core strength  and core stability exercise so lots of  people do  tons and tons of plank they'll stay in  plank position for minutes at a time  trying to train their core and this is  useful because it trains your core  endurance which is something missing  from a lot of people the important thing  with your cause it needs to work all  throughout the day to keep you upright  and as that gets tired you start to  introduce the possibility of injury so  it is useful to train in that manner but  the problem with the plank as other  people have pointed out is not only that  it's relatively very easy and you don't  get that much bang for your buck but  also that you're training anti-extension  that in other words you're using the  rectus abdomini the muscle on the front  of your stomach to curl yourself inwards  to prevent your body from bending that  away and the problem with that is not  that it's unimportant but rather that  it's something that we already get an  awful lot of already particularly if you  do lots of push-ups where you're already  in that position so what's the point of  doing loads of push-ups and then doing  plank you're essentially holding the  same position with your torso seeing as  we sit in a hunched position all day  seeing as we do tons of crunches and not  so many back extension exercises we'd  actually need more strength that way we  need anti-flexion if we're gonna avoid  injuries particularly in the lower back  and this is where the magic of the body  weight row comes in because when you're  in this position and your heels are  touching the ground you're preventing  your body from contorting that way the  opposite direction once again this makes  it the perfect counterbalance that you  can use to strengthen the back of your  torso the back of your core your erector  spinae in order to make sure that they  have the strength endurance to keep you  upright throughout the day so don't hurt  yourself when you bend over to pick  something up and because you're doing  rows you're also getting the lat workout  and the biceps workout and the traps so  you're getting loads of bang for your  buck you're just sneaking in a fantastic  core stability exercise in there that  will improve your athleticism and your  resilience to injury of course this  doesn't work as well with the front  lever which you think would be the  perfect harder progression from the  bodyweight row but when you lift your  legs off the ground then suddenly it  once again becomes anti-extension  because now you're fighting gravity  which is pulling your legs back down  towards the ground so even if you can do  front lever it doesn't replace the body  weight row you should still be doing the  bodyweight row and that's not all the  other great thing about the bodyweight  row is it allows you to practice your  scapular attraction that means putting  the shoulder blades backwards like that  and what you don't want to do when  you're in the bottom of the movement is  just let them sag like so i mean it's  not gonna be the end of the world but  this is a fantastic opportunity to train  something that has loads of transfer to  other movements like the front lever  because in the front lever you need to  keep that scapular pull backwards so  that you can keep yourself nice and  rigid and not flop about and fail to  hold the position so this is a great way  to build up the strength you need for  more advanced movements and likewise  this is something that a lot of people  have gotten how to do simply because  they don't move their shoulder blades  all that much and by training this  you'll unlock additional strength and  performance and mobility that you  otherwise might have lost so another  fantastic reason to practice this you  can also just hold yourself in position  like that  for extra credit if you want to make it  even more difficult then you can also  try  using one arm and adding a rotation a  one arm bodyweight row with rotation  pulling yourself up and twisting to  touch the air as you go and the reason  that this is so useful is because this  is a movement pattern once again that we  use a lot in real life whether you're  opening a door like i say whether you're  pulling anything else towards you  whether you're wrestling you don't tend  to just pull with a static torso you  tend to pull and twist at the same time  alternatively you can try doing this  whilst keeping your torso rigid which  makes it a fantastic anti-rotation  movement once again brilliant for core  stability so there's so many aspects  this movement is so easy anyone can do  it but i never see anyone doing it so  there you go try incorporating  bodyweight rows into your routine i hope  you found this video useful and  interesting let me know in the comments  down below if you'd like me to do more  like this where i discuss a particular  movement and if you would i have lots  more options available we've got lots  more on the way all the usual stuff i'm  going to be talking about saitama one  punch man training very soon and the  mind muscle connection so if that sounds  good then subscribe and stick around of  course i've got a book that's available  for pre-order right now called  functional training and beyond which  talks about concepts like this the  functional aspects of training and how  to make your training more functional  than it currently is if you're following  a kind of bro split to a power lifting  routine  this is the stuff that will help to  perform better and avoid injury and be  stronger in your day-to-day life so that  sounds cool then pre-order that  alternatively i also have my own ebook  and training program super functional  training that takes a lot of these  concepts and builds them into a single  program that you can follow that trains  your body and mind your mobility and  your speed your strength and your power  so if all that sounds great then that's  also in the link in the description down  below thanks so much for watching this  one guys and i'll see you next time  thanks a ton for watching  bye for now

